## TL;DR  

1. **Host a CJK web-font** (e.g. `NotoSansSC-Regular.otf`).  
2. **Add a `font-faces` entry** that maps the *same* `text-font` name you already use (`"Roboto Regular"`) to that CJK file **only for the CJK Unicode ranges**.  
3. **Leave the `glyphs` URL untouched** – it will still supply the Latin glyphs from the Roboto PBFs.  
4. **Load the `font-faces` object into the map** (static in the style JSON or dynamically with `map.setFontFaces()`).

That’s it – Chinese place-names will be drawn with Noto Sans SC, while all Latin labels keep the Roboto look.

---

## 1. Why this works

| What the style does today | What we add |
|---------------------------|-------------|
| `glyphs` URL → SDF PBFs for **Roboto** (Latin, Greek, Cyrillic, etc.) | `font-faces` → tells MapLibre “when you see a code-point that belongs to the CJK ranges, use this *local* font file instead of the glyph PBF”. |
| `text-font: ["Roboto Regular"]` on every label layer | No change – the same font-stack name is still used, so you don’t have to touch any layer definitions. |
| CJK characters fall back to the browser’s default `sans-serif` (because you have no CJK glyphs) | The `font-faces` entry overrides that fallback **only for the CJK ranges**, pulling in the Noto Sans SC file you host. |

MapLibre GL JS ≥ 6.7 reads `font-faces`. When a character’s Unicode code-point matches a `unicode-range` entry, the corresponding font file is fetched (via the browser) and used for shaping/rendering. All other characters continue to be rendered from the glyph PBFs supplied by the `glyphs` URL.

---

## 2. Host the CJK font file

1. Download the regular weight of Noto Sans SC (or any other CJK-compatible OTF/TTF).  
   ```text
   https://github.com/googlefonts/noto-cjk/raw/main/Sans/OTF/TraditionalChinese/NotoSansSC-Regular.otf
   ```
2. Put it on a web-accessible location that serves **CORS** headers (`Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` or your domain).  
   Example URL: `https://example.com/fonts/NotoSansSC-Regular.otf`

> **Tip:** If you already have a CDN or static-file bucket, just drop the file there. The file size is ~1 MB, which is fine for a one-time download per client.

---

## 3. Add a `font-faces` block to the style

You can edit the style JSON directly (static) **or** add it at runtime with `map.setFontFaces()`.  
Below is the static-JSON version; it only adds the CJK entry – you don’t need to list the Roboto file because the glyph server already covers Latin.

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "glyphs": "https://demotiles.maplibre.org/font/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf",
  "font-faces": {
    "Roboto Regular": [
      {
        "url": "https://example.com/fonts/NotoSansSC-Regular.otf",
        "unicode-range": [
          "U+3000-30FF",   // CJK Symbols & Punctuation
          "U+3400-4DBF",   // CJK Unified Ideographs Extension A
          "U+4E00-9FFF",   // CJK Unified Ideographs
          "U+F900-FAFF",   // CJK Compatibility Ideographs
          "U+20000-2FA1F"  // CJK Unified Ideographs Extension B-F
        ]
      }
    ]
  },

  "layers": [
    {
      "id": "place-labels",
      "type": "symbol",
      "source": "openmaptiles",
      "source-layer": "place",
      "layout": {
        "text-field": ["coalesce", ["get", "name:zh"], ["get", "name"]],
        "text-font": ["Roboto Regular"],
        "text-size": 12
      }
    }
    /* … other layers … */
  ]
}
```

### What the block does

* **Key** – `"Roboto Regular"` matches the `text-font` you already use.  
* **Array value** – you can list *multiple* font-file objects for the same name. Here we only add one, the CJK file.  
* **`unicode-range`** – limits the file to the CJK blocks. Anything outside those ranges (Latin, Greek, Cyrillic, etc.) will still be taken from the glyph PBFs served by the `glyphs` URL.

> **Optional:** If you also want to serve a local Roboto file (e.g. for offline use), add a second object with a `unicode-range` that covers the Latin blocks (`U+0000-00FF`, `U+0100-024F`, …). The two objects will be tried in order, so the first matching range wins.

---

## 4. Loading the `font-faces` at runtime (optional)

If you cannot (or don’t want to) bake the block into the style file, you can inject it after the map is created:

```javascript
import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';

const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://example.com/your-style.json',
  center: [116.4, 39.9],
  zoom: 5
});

map.on('style.load', () => {
  const fontFaces = {
    "Roboto Regular": [
      {
        url: "https://example.com/fonts/NotoSansSC-Regular.otf",
        "unicode-range": [
          "U+3000-30FF",
          "U+3400-4DBF",
          "U+4E00-9FFF",
          "U+F900-FAFF",
          "U+20000-2FA1F"
        ]
      }
    ]
  };

  // Apply the mapping; GL-JS will fetch the font file on demand.
  map.setFontFaces(fontFaces);
});
```

*`map.setFontFaces()`* replaces (or merges with) any existing `font-faces` in the style, so you can call it later to swap fonts without re-loading the whole style.

---

## 5. Verify it works

1. Open the map in the browser.  
2. Open DevTools → **Network** tab, filter by **font**. You should see a request for `NotoSansSC-Regular.otf`.  
3. Chinese place names should now appear in the Noto Sans SC design (notice the slightly different stroke width and glyph shapes).  
4. Latin labels should look exactly the same as before (still rendered from the Roboto glyph PBFs).

If you still see the default `sans-serif`:

* Check the console for errors like *“Failed to load font …”* – most often a CORS problem.  
* Make sure the Unicode ranges cover the characters you’re testing (you can temporarily remove the `unicode-range` array to see if the font is applied at all).  
* Ensure the style’s `text-font` is still exactly `"Roboto Regular"` (case-sensitive, no extra spaces).

---

## 6. What about `localIdeographFontFamily`?

`localIdeographFontFamily` only affects **layers that keep the default `text-font`** (i.e. they don’t specify a custom `text-font`). Since you *do* specify `"Roboto Regular"` on every label layer, that option does **not** apply. The `font-faces` approach is the correct way to override the CJK rendering while preserving the existing font stack.

If you ever decide to **remove the `glyphs` URL completely** and rely on *only* local/web fonts, you could set:

```javascript
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  // …
  glyphs: undefined,               // omit or set to null
  localIdeographFontFamily: '"Noto Sans SC", sans-serif'
});
```

But that would also replace the Latin glyphs with whatever local font the browser finds for Roboto, which is not what you want here.

---

## 7. Native SDKs (optional note)

MapLibre **Native** (Android / iOS) reads the same `font-faces` property (Android 11.13+, iOS 6.18+). The only extra step is to make sure the URLs you give are reachable from the device (e.g. bundle the OTF in the app and serve it via a `file://` URL, or host it on a server with proper CORS). The rest of the workflow is identical.

---

